    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nCython seems to break function overloading when sub-classing a Pydantic model and defining a new overloaded method implementation. This only seems to happen when subclassing an uncompiled Pydantic class in a compiled module, and only when overriding an underscore prefixed method (such as _test). I presume this is some interaction between the metaclass and Cython, but results in a pretty significant behavior divergence when compared to raw python.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\nUncompiled module\n```python\nimport pydantic\nfrom pydantic import ConfigDict\n\n\nclass Test(pydantic.BaseModel):\n    model_config = ConfigDict(\n        arbitrary_types_allowed=True,\n        extra=\"forbid\",\n    )\n\n    name: str\n\n    def _test(self) -> int:\n        raise NotImplementedError(f\"Unimplemented in class {type(self)}\")\n```\n\nCythonized module:\n```cython\nfrom uncythonized.base import Test\n\n\nclass Test2(Test):\n    def _test(self) -> int:\n        return 1\n\nt = Test2(name=\"test\")\nprint(t._test())\n```\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nThe expected behavior, and the one that occurs when running this in pure python, is that the subclasses implementation of _test should be used, and thus the example code should print `1`. Instead, the observed behavior is that the superclass's implementation is used instead, leading to an error:\n```\nNotImplementedError: Unimplemented in class <class 'cython_test.Test2'>\n```\n\n### OS\n\nLinux (Ubuntu 24.04 LTS)\n\n### Python version\n\n3.12.8 (managed through uv 0.13.5)\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.3.1\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_",
